  "transcript": "Before the school group arrives, I'd like to explain how we're going to run this morning's session. The aim is to create a relaxed atmosphere, so please let the children suggest answers before you explain anything. We want them to notice evidence, not feel they're being tested.\n\nStart with the model of the Earth. Point to the crust, the outermost layer, and explain that we usually learn about things below it indirectly. At the core of this session is a simple idea: evidence can tell us about something we can't see for ourselves.\n\nYou can demonstrate that idea with the sealed gas containers on the same table. The first contains oxygen. Its appearance alone won't distinguish it from ordinary air, so explain that scientists use suitable tests instead. The other is labelled hydrogen. We aren't going to open either container; use the short video to show how scientists identify their contents.\n\nNext, compare a clean piece of iron with a rusty one. Explain that a reaction has produced an oxide. This is a useful example of a change we can observe directly. Before the group leaves, show the room's carbon dioxide monitor. Ask why that reading might change as more people come inside. You don't need to give them every answer. Leave them with a question they can think about on the journey home.\n"
}
